    "acquired_at": "2026-11-03T23:07:25.057708+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-03T23:07:25.057708+00:00",
    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • SRT Performance Joins the 2026 SEMA Show Featuring the First-Ever Jeep Wrangler Scrambler Concept\nNew Matchbox Collector Series Cars for 2027: Is It Time to Lose Hope?\n24 photos\nPhoto: Lamley Group\nFor the first time in weeks, one of my local toy stores just brought in a new case of Hot Wheels Mainline models. I wasn’t exactly sure which one it was though, as I couldn’t recall where the green Mazda RX-7, silver Audi R8 Convertible and blue Ferrari 12Cilindri belonged.\nI found the answer the moment I spotted the black Nissan Skyline HT 2000GT-X, which was the official Treasure Hunt collectible for this year’s Case P. Yes, that’s how far behind we are right now. I just showed you a preview of 2027’s Case C about a week ago, and the stores here aren’t even done with 2026 yet. I also spoke to one of my dealers to keep an old Impala safe for me until he brings in more cars.\nIt’s an item I don’t even remember seeing before, and you’ll be shocked by the size of the wheels. Now, I’m debating whether I should order 10 more Matchbox Moving Parts models from a buddy of mine I haven’t worked with in a while.\nHe’s the only person who has them in stock in a 300-mile radius, and I’ve often loved unboxing cars from that series. I can’t say the same thing about the Collector Series though, which seems stuck in a loop that it may never get out of.\nSure, they all have True Grip tires, but the wheels are often disappointing. And that’s not the worst part. I realize having moving parts on all of them might jeopardize Mattel’s Hot Wheels Premium lines. But having us guess all the time which models are “sealed” and which aren’t has become tedious. Now, Mr. Lambert seems to think this case will be the only one we’ll get in 2027, just because of the numbering system.\nPhoto: Lamley Group Matchbox usually used x/20 as opposed to what you’ll see in Car Culture (1/5). I think it’s highly improbable that the die cast manufacturer is planning to retire this series: this is change is probably meant to avoid any confusion for the future. You’ll spot six items inside, and there’s no new casting this time. The 1990 Corvette ZR1 debuted earlier this year (#8 of 20) with a simple red finish and gray 5-spoke wheels.\nSeeing it in yellow with a few decals should make it more enticing, but I can’t stand black wheels and the stance seems off, too. I’m not saying I’ll 100% ignore it if I find one in the wild, but it’s not my favorite model in this case.\nThe 2007 Land Rover 6x6 is available both as a basic release and a Moving Parts collectible. It should have an opening hood which is a bonus. Chances are it will be the first one to fly off the pegs, as we don’t have that many 6x6 options to choose from anyway.\nItem #3 of 6 for the mix is the 1988 Porsche 911 Turbo Slant Nose which looked fairly promising in black. It has had a metal base since day one, and I confess I’m surprised to see the Promotional Toy Show Leipzig variation in this video as Mattel only made 250 of them.\nPhoto: Lamley Group The new color combo looks dull at best, although I suspect it could be derived from a real-world livery. The 1993 Jaguar XJ220s first showed up in 2025, and I was almost convinced I’d get one.\nIt all changed when I saw it live, and I’ve ignored it twice so far. Third time’s a charm, right? This light blue isn’t the first color that comes to mind when thinking about the British supercar, which isn’t to say it doesn’t look look brilliant on it.\nI almost don’t mind the wheels either. Having the 1984 Audi Quattro Sport S1 in here is slightly strange, given it was inside the same case with the Jaguar this year as well.\nIt’s sporting a different livery this time, and I might buy it just so I can do a close-up comparison with the one from Tarmac Works . The Datsun 240Z Rally is #4 of 6 for the set, and this is its second iteration so far.\nPhoto: Lamley Group To me, that’s further evidence that Mattel won’t pull the plug on the series just yet, as it wouldn’t be a financially-wise decision considering the licensing costs involved. Is it time to lose hope in this series?\nI would think not, especially since it has delivered quite a few collectibles over the past few years. Think back at 2022’s 1963 Honda T360, 1935 Ford Pickup and 1964 Chevy C10 Longbed. One year later Mattel revealed the 1979 Freightliner FLT (which never got a second chance) and the 1953 Ford COE (which I unboxed a variation of last year).\nThings seemed to have started to go downhill in 2024, but I can assure you 2025’s 1978 Ford Bronco is a real hit! I’ll give you a taste of a special Autozam soon!\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nmatchbox Porsche 911 datsun 240z Jaguar XJ220 Chevrolet Corvette land rover Audi Mattel\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT Q&A: Fresh Electric Dirt Bike Player, Yozma, Is Attending EICMA With New IN11 – Here's What's Coming",
